 For each task, it is possible that a single sentence summary will not be sufficient to cover all the questions (e.g. for question 1, a longer summary would also need to refer to 'Twitter'). For those cases, the model should not be penalized if its answer is longer (up to a maximum of 25 words). Each question-summary pair will be a separate text document. The dataset will be released.

\section{Discussion and Future Work}
\label{section:discussion}
We propose a framework for automatically generating question-summary pairs, which allows for a number of possible extensions:

\end{question}

\subsection{System}

Our system relies on the pretrained BERT model \citep{bert}, fine-tuned on a training dataset composed of pairs of sentences. The output of BERT is then processed using a linear layer and a dropout layer. The linear layer maps the BERT output to a classification space that corresponds to the labels in the dataset. The dropout layer is used to reduce the number of parameters and mitigate the problem of overfitting.
